# State coverage page layout (locked)

California (`estados/california.html` + `en/states/california.html`) is the **canonical state page** — including expense buckets (three by default, **four** when `WHY_FINAL_EXPENSE` is set for that state), large package totals after charts, and stacked deep-dive links. Every licensed-state coverage page uses this format. Texas (`estados/texas.html`) is the reference for the optional fourth bucket.

Do not invent a denser hero, put package totals above the charts, widen the cost tables, inline link pairs with `·`, or repeat the same outbound teaching-page URL in more than one bucket.

## Section order (do not reorder)

1. **Older-Adult-First hero** (`sc-hero--answer-first`) — H1, one short lead, **two** bullets max, one CTA. No cost numbers, carrier lists, or long how-it-works in the hero.
2. **Hero agent bar** — this state’s license only (photo, name, license #, NPN, View license, Verify). Full roster stays on `licencias.html` / `en/licenses.html`.
3. **Funeral / final expense costs** — one H2 with **three expense buckets**, plus an optional **fourth** (see below)
4. **Carriers** Mejor Vida can compare in that state
5. **How final expense works** (short bullets)
6. **Navy quote band**
7. **Other state / city links** when present

## Hero (locked)

- Class: `sc-hero sc-hero--answer-first sc-hero--{code}`
- Lead names the **agency** (Mejor Vida Insurance / Mejor Vida Seguros), not Julie’s first name in body CTAs
- Bullets: free quote by age/health/budget + licensed in **this state** · NPN only (do not list other licensed states)
- One gold CTA to quote; license proof lives in the agent bar under the hero
- Transparent cutout heroes (like CA/NV/**TX**) keep the same thin answer-first copy — do not refill the hero with costs
- **Texas hero art:** `node scripts/build-texas-state-hero.js` → `img/state-heroes/texas-hero.png` → `npm run optimize:images` (map from state GeoJSON + official seal SVG). CSS: `.sc-hero--tx` in `css/state-coverage.css`. Bump `heroVersion("texas")` after asset changes.
- **License PDF pending address:** If the state certificate PDF must not be public yet, omit `pdf` in `LICENSE` / `js/mvi-licensing-map.js` and use `pdfPending: true` + `verifyUrl` (modal shows verify panel, not a PDF).

## Cost section — expense buckets (locked)

Built by `expenseCostSections()` + `whyFinalExpenseBlock()` in `scripts/render-state-coverage-pages.js`.

Under **How much does a funeral cost in {state}?** / **¿Cuánto cuesta un funeral en {state}?**:

0. **Overview TOC** — short line that the bill has more than one part, then an ordered list linking to each bucket (**three** or **four** items when `WHY_FINAL_EXPENSE[code]` exists)
1. **Funeral home costs** — statewide Funeralocity package charts; package total **after** each table in large type; source + third-party note; then **one** stacked link list (`.sc-expense-links`) to the funeral-cost guide **and** the estimator
2. **Cemetery: plot, vault, and marker** — brief plain-language definitions of each; opening/closing called out as separate; published plot examples / resale cards when we have state data. **No** second link to the funeral-cost guide
3. **Other expenses** — short list (credit cards, mortgage/rent, medical bills, small loans); then a stacked `.sc-expense-links` list to `blog/que-son-gastos-finales.html` / `en/what-are-final-expenses.html` and the coverage-sizing page — do **not** expand into a full essay here
4. **Why final expense insurance** (only when `WHY_FINAL_EXPENSE[code]` is defined) — `.sc-expense-block--why`, id `why-final-expense` / `por-que-gastos-finales`. Short state-specific copy (probate delay, funeral due at time of service, beneficiary payment vs waiting on the estate). **One** primary CTA button to `quote.html` (`.sc-expense-why-cta`). Do not turn this into a long article.

### Fourth bucket — locked TOC labels (family protection, not probate in the TOC)

When the fourth bucket is present, the overview says **four** groups and the TOC fourth line uses **only** these sublines (do not swap to “probate” wording in the TOC):

| Language | TOC line |
|----------|----------|
| EN | `Why final expense insurance` — **the easiest way to protect your family** |
| ES | `Por qué un seguro de gastos finales` — **la forma más sencilla de proteger a su familia** |

Add or edit body copy in the `WHY_FINAL_EXPENSE` object in `render-state-coverage-pages.js` (ES + EN `paragraphs` + `cta`). Texas (`TX`) is the first entry; copy other states from that pattern with accurate local probate facts — no invented timelines or court fees.

Keep each bucket short. Deep detail lives on the linked teaching pages.

### Outbound links (locked)

- Use `<ul class="sc-expense-links">` with one `<li>` per link — **stacked vertically** on all viewports (especially mobile). Never place two teaching links on one line with a middle dot (`·`).
- Do **not** repeat the same outbound page URL in more than one expense bucket.
- Funeral-home bucket is the only place that links to `cuanto-cuesta-un-funeral.html` / `en/how-much-does-a-funeral-cost.html`.

### Each funeral-home package block

1. Package title (traditional burial → full-service cremation → affordable burial → direct cremation)
2. Narrow line-item table (SERVICE TYPE / LOW / HIGH / AVERAGE)
3. **Package total after the table** — large Low / High / Average (`.sc-cost-package-total`); Average is the visual emphasis

Do **not** put package totals above the chart. Do **not** merge cemetery or other-debt copy back into the funeral-home intro.

### Cost layout CSS

- Cost / expense column max-width **42rem** (`.sc-cost-section-container`, `.sc-expense-overview`, `.sc-expense-block-inner`)
- Stacked links: `.sc-expense-links` in `css/state-coverage.css`
- Shared styles only — do not invent per-state cost table CSS

## Data source (locked)

- Funeralocity state averages via `scripts/harvest-funeralocity-state-costs.js`
- Detailed + short: `integrations/knowledge/Funeralocity_State_Costs/all-states-detailed.json`
- Licensed summary: `ne-ks-co-nv.json` (includes all currently licensed states, including CA)
- Map short averages: `js/state-coverage-costs.js`
- Re-harvest before claiming numbers are current; cite Funeralocity on the page

## Builders

- Template: `scripts/render-state-coverage-pages.js` — add the state to `SLUGS` + `LICENSE`, optional `WHY_FINAL_EXPENSE[code]`, then rebuild
- Map + license modal: `js/state-coverage-costs.js`, `js/mvi-licensing-map.js`; harvest licensed states in `scripts/harvest-funeralocity-state-costs.js` (`ne-ks-co-nv.json`)
- After cost or layout changes: `node scripts/render-state-coverage-pages.js` (ES + EN)
- Do **not** hand-edit generated state HTML for layout; change the builder and rebuild
- Mirror ES + EN. Spanish indexable; English `noindex` (existing pattern)

## Do not

- List the full licensed-state roster on the state page body (licenses pages only)
- Put funeral prices or premiums in the hero H1 or bullets
- Move package totals back above the charts
- Widen cost tables past the locked ~42rem column
- Inline deep-dive links side by side (use stacked `.sc-expense-links`)
- Duplicate the funeral-cost guide link under the cemetery bucket
- Turn the cemetery, “other expenses”, or “why final expense” buckets into long articles — link out instead
- Change the locked fourth-bucket TOC subline to probate/legal jargon (protection framing stays in the TOC; probate detail stays in the bucket body only)
- Use Julie’s first name in body CTAs (agency name only; Julie stays in the agent bar / license line)
- Invent Funeralocity numbers — harvest or cite the stored harvest
